0a373ca87142,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/0a373ca87142.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/0a373ca87142.pdf,60dd6a03e18af8d938c619d802b2f2c405e64120b080b6df8e64439d75dfe6a4,True,32,"Surveying a Landscape of Ethics-Focused Design Methods SHRUTHI SAI CHI V UK ULA , Pratt Institute, New York City, United States COLIN GRAY , Indiana University, Bloomington, United States ZIQING LI , Purdue University, West Lafayette, United States ANNE C. PIVONKA , Purdue University, West Lafayette, United States JINGNING CHEN , Purdue University, West Lafayette, United States Over the past decade, HCI researchers and practitioners have increasingly addressed ethics-focused issues through a range of theoretical, methodological, and pragmatic contributions to the field. While many forms of design knowledge have been proposed and described, we focus explicitly on knowledge that has been codified as “methods,” which we define as structured supports for everyday work practices of designers. In this article, we identify, analyze, and map a collection of 63 existing methods intentionally designed for ethical impact. Building on results of a content analysis of these methods, we contribute a"
0b002633de00,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/0b002633de00.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/0b002633de00.pdf,c9421685d160f4a802535b030d61e31a4891aa9e3811215cfdd27aa922b7853b,True,8,"A SECURITY DESIGN PA TTERN TAXONOMY BASED ON A TTACK PA TTERNS Findings of a Systematic Literature Review Andreas Wiesauer, Johannes Sametinger Department of Business Informatics - Software Engineering, Johannes Kepler University of Linz, Altenbergerstrasse 69, Linz, Austria andreas.wiesauer@jku.at, johannes.sametinger@jku.at Keywords: security design pattern taxonomy, security design patterns, attack patterns, literature review Abstract: Security design patterns are proven solutions to security problems in a given context with constructive mea- sures of how to design certain parts of a software system. The literature contains numerous deﬁnitions, ex- amples, and taxonomies of such patterns. There are also a few quality criteria for them. We suggest a new taxonomy based on attack patterns in order to enhance applicability of security design patterns especially for non-experts in software security. We further suggest a combined consideration of attack patterns, security design patterns "
