8e0dfec2492d,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/8e0dfec2492d.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/8e0dfec2492d.pdf,926814ea2cdc0496c5717ecbc312fe8fce22c7bf4daf3195cd2e089c38b30124,True,5,"Drama and Software Patterns Valentino Vrani´c Faculty of Informatics Pan-European University Bratislava, Slovakia valentino.vranic@paneurouni.com Abstract—Patterns are recurring forms that generate structure by resolving conflicts of contradicting forces present in the context in which they are applied. First described as an approach to capturing building architecture knowledge by Alexander, patterns were extensively adopted in software development and also in other areas of human practice. Many patterns have been described and many different formats have been used for this. Instead of “describing patterns,” many authors even say “writing patterns.” However, a description of a pattern is not the pattern itself. Consequently, it does not bring an imminent understanding of the pattern. On the other hand, when looking at patterns directly, it is difficult to see their essence in all the details of the context in which they are applied. Drama and a particular kind of organizational pattern"
8eb8539b9712,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/8eb8539b9712.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/8eb8539b9712.pdf,93bd77110d65f74db3f8bceaf9ab8d09f2bba3474550d150320bbe3f75b65b38,True,5,"Designing Dialogic Disclaimers: Principles for Ethical Conversational Design in LLM Interfaces Theodore H Cheng New York University New York City, New York, USA hc3652@nyu.edu Abstract Current disclaimer approaches in large language model (LLM) sys- tems often disrupt conversational flow with static, one-shot warn- ings that fail to engage users in deeper ethical reflection and, as a growing body of evidence reveals, are largely ineffective at achiev- ing their intended goals of calibrated trust and informed use. This paper examines how disclaimers can be reimagined as integrated dialogic elements that maintain conversational coherence while effectively communicating necessary ethical information. Through a theoretical synthesis drawing on research in conversational re- pair, dialogue structure, AI transparency, and empirical studies of user interaction with LLMs, we identify four design principles for dialogic disclaimers: Conversational Continuity, Contextual Adaptation, Layered Comm"
8f065cfd18a4,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/8f065cfd18a4.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/8f065cfd18a4.pdf,d28612d25d82b1980c9e8ae2710323e46bac9c4e80fe2b2ddcb5d62e4bd47db0,True,9,"Pattern language: A framework for learning Alan Jessop * University of Durham Business School, Mil Hill Lane, Durham DH1 3LB, UK Abstract Pattern languages (PLs) were devised for use in architecture and urban design as ways of providing useful access to design knowledge both for the expert designer and for the lay user.They have subsequently found use in the software community for technical and organisational issues.The paper reports some applications of PLs: (1) as a vehicle for making explicit knowledge that would otherwise remain tacit; (2) as an aid to teaching; (3) as a means of presenting statistical methods to managers. /C2112003 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved. Keywords: Knowledge-based systems; Pattern language; Learning 1. Introduction There exists an apparently paradoxical situa- tion in which, on the one hand, it is proposed that organisations should be learning organisations, and, on the other, that individual creativity should be prized and encouraged.Whatever organisa-"
9020aa145571,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/9020aa145571.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/9020aa145571.pdf,c541f9c116bd45d9936d58b7abfb27a5d0003734b3fd2dd5dfa1e2223ec987ad,True,16,"A Process Framework for Designing Software Reference Architectures for providing Tools as a Service Muhammad Aufeef Chauhan*±1,3, Muhammad Ali Babar±1,2, Christian W. Probst3 ±CREST-Centre for Research on Engineering Software Technologies1,2 1Software and Systems Section, IT University of Copenhagen, Denmark 2The University of Adelaide, Australia 3Formal Methods Section, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Technical University of Denmark, Denmark muac@itu.dk, ali.babar@adelaide.edu.au, cwpr@dtu.dk Abstract. Software Reference Architecture (SRA), which is a generic architec-ture solution for a specific type of software systems, provides foundation for the design of concrete architectures in terms of architecture design guidelines and architecture elements. The complexity and size of certain types of software sys-tems need customized and systematic SRA design and evaluation methods. In this paper, we present a software Reference Architecture Design process Framework ("
9049c4d2bbec,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/9049c4d2bbec.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/9049c4d2bbec.pdf,9ae732311ef33b3eaeb2618e74eb78eca22df5ba6fc372cf65a3020e98331961,True,13,"J. Fong, R. Kwan, and F.L. Wang (Eds.): ICHL 2008, LNCS 5169, pp. 66–78, 2008. © Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2008 Towards a Design Theory of Blended Learning Curriculum Ronghuai Huang1, Ding Ma1,2, and Haisen Zhang1,3 1 Beijing Normal University, Beijing, 100875, China huangrh@bnu.edu.cn 2 Chinese People’s Police Security University, Beijing, 100038, China mading70@126.com 3 University of International Business and Economics, Beijing, 100029, China haisenzhang@uibe.edu.cn Abstract. The purpose of this article is to develop a design theory of blended learning curriculum in ways of establishing a model for designing such a cur- riculum and a model for designing an activity in a blended learning curriculum as well as demonstrating how these models can be utilized in a curriculum design. It first attempts to define what the essence of blended learning is by drawing on definitions of previous studies. Then, it goes on to identify the char- acteristics and rationales of blended learnin"
