c5d4c66275a8,frozen_450,/Users/baiyixin/Downloads/pdf_386_by_paper_id/c5d4c66275a8.pdf,/Users/baiyixin/Documents/Survey - design knowledge/expanded_pdf_corpus_459_20260811/c5d4c66275a8.pdf,48a104e8bbbc09cc16d77e96fec64674e321a23fd7c10101d1296f9fab30eafd,True,13,"TOWAR DS A THEORY DRIVEN FOUNDATIONAL MODEL FOR DESIGN: RETHINKING REQUIREMENT ELICITATION FOR SPECIAL NEEDS Kavyashree Venkatesh PhD Scholar, Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, India Shakuntala Acharya Assistant Professor Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, India Janet K. Allen John and Mary Moore Chair and Professor University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, USA Farrokh Mistree L.A. Comp Chair and Professor University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, USA ABSTRACT Designing assistive devices involves complex interactions between design knowledge structures, requirement elicitation methods, and stakeholder engagement. Current co -design approaches, while effective, rely heavily on implicit knowledge transfer, particul arly when designing for users unable to articulate their needs. In this paper we present the theoretical underpinnings of requirement elicitation as a design knowledge transformation process. It requires a comprehensive understanding of the special needs of the users, critica"
